first of all let me say I regret deeply  the need to drive efficiency very hard  and that it will have an impact on jobs  in centrica and that we announced today  as you say another 4,000 jobs the driver  for this efficiency program are three  things really the first is actually what  our customers want and that our  customers are asking to go more digital  the the channels in which we reach  customers are therefore changing the  costs of those channels tend to be lower  and unfortunately they use fewer people  the second big driver is about  competitive pressure where we've got to  keep driving to be the most efficient  price setter that we can consistent with  the level of service we want to give and  the third driver is this impending price  cap so I'm afraid there is a linkage  between this price cap and the need to  drive efficiency and these job losses  but you've been quite clear there these  measures have been forced on you partly  because of the government's price cap  why is that  well these measures have been have been  forced on us in part by but not only by  the impending price cap why is that  because if our prices are going to be  capped then we clearly need to drive  costs as hard as we can the government  indeed say that's one of their stated  outcomes they want to achieve through  this price cap we believe that the  analysis is fundamentally flawed in the  supporting this price cap but more  worryingly we actually think a price  caps can be bad for customers if you  look at the one that's been implemented  already in the prepayment market we've  ended up with most prices within ten  pounds of each other it's a little bit  like university tuition fees everyone  now is charging the same as the cap it's  reducing competition and we think it's  actually going to be bad so there's  quite a lot going on in the market right  now and power and SSE merging their  household supply businesses she'll have  just come into the market via its  purchase of first utility how  do you think the competitive landscape  is gonna get for you well look first of  all this market is extremely competitive  already there are 67 suppliers so adding  one or two or if you like adding one in  the form of shell and taking one away  through the proposed consolidation of  two of our competitors means it'll stay  about the same number competitions a  good thing it's good for customers we  shouldn't fear it it makes us sharper  now there's been a lot of response to  the statement today from the unions from  the Labour Party the Labour Party again  talking about nationalization of assets  how concerned are you about that look I  realize that people get very concerned  when job losses are announced and and  clearly with our colleagues in the  unions I mean we've already been working  with them we we are actually aligned on  some aspects of of energy policy with  the unions but clearly they're going to  be standing up for their members here  this is the beginning of a long  conversation with them it's not even  clear yet how many of the job losses  will necessarily be an individual union  represented group so I think it's  probably a little bit premature but I  respect their position as far as the  Labour Party's concerned I believe that  we need to engage with them as we do all  parties we always do and we are and  they're entitled to their their views we  believe that competitive markets are the  way to go in energy and I will be  engaging with Rebecca long Bailey and  the other colleagues in the Labour Party  as we walk forward do you think the  share price performance over the last 12  months reflects concerns over  nationalization  I think the share price performance over  the last 12 months clearly reflects much  more than our cash flow and performance  because although you know people can  argue about them we did meet our  published 2017 targets we did have a  performance issue but not known until  the fourth quarter and most of the  decline